; a.b.c.example. is the only name here with any records.  c.example. and
; b.c.example. exist because it does -- they are empty non-terminals,
; and NOERROR with nothing in it is what each of them is owed.
;
; The configuration has the server deny them instead.  gouv.fr answered
; an empty non-terminal that way and left a TXT record saying "broken
; ENT was here"; what it costs depends on the resolver asking, and a
; resolver which walks down a name one label at a time is the one with
; most to lose.
$ORIGIN example.
$TTL 3600
@	IN	SOA	ns.example. root.example. 1 3600 900 604800 3600
@	IN	NS	ns.example.
ns	IN	{{AUTH_ADDR_RR}}
a.b.c	IN	A	192.0.2.1
